{
  "schema_version": 2,
  "source": "# Harbor railway operations\n\nThe control panel is in the eastern cabin. Block signals prevent two trains from entering the same occupied track section. A red aspect tells the driver to stop.\n\nThe detector cable follows the north rail. A failed detector is treated as an occupied block until inspected. Conservative detector handling preserves safe separation between trains.\n\nDay tickets permit unlimited journeys on the harbor line until midnight. They cost twelve tokens for adults. Children under six travel free with an adult.\n\nThe ticket machine accepts copper tokens. Unused day tickets are refundable before their printed travel date. Used tickets cannot be refunded.\n\nA portable radio is kept beside the panel. During a signal failure, a controller issues written movement authority for one train at a time. Written authority provides controlled movement when signals are unavailable.\n\nSignal restoration requires a functional test before normal traffic resumes. The technician records the test in the cabin log. The controller signs the entry after seeing the test results.\n",
  "source_name": "rail.md",
